WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.079 --> 00:00:01.102
(fireworks crackling)

00:00:01.102 --> 00:00:03.950
- [Narrator] There's a big trend
right now in the IPO world.

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:07.000
- Be latest company to go
public through a SPAC IPO.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.900
- SPACs for many, many
years had a very bad name.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.490
- [Narrator] Over the last two years,

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:15.630
companies like Nikola,
DraftKings and Virgin Galactic

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.740
have all entered the market
through something called SPACs,

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:21.210
or Special Purpose Acquisition Companies.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.680
These controversial entities

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.380
also known as blank-check
companies offer private businesses

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.140
a fast track to going public

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.310
that bypasses the traditional IPO process.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:33.130
And they're growing more popular.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:37.100
You can see activity has almost
quadrupled so far in 2020.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.010
Some investors see a
big opportunity in SPACs

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.840
and say they're a more efficient way

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.350
for companies to go public.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.960
But critics say they
allow for backdoor deals

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.820
and that they're not worth the risk.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.060
- One criticism of the
SPAC process is that

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.950
investors can get into a
company and then get burned,

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:54.870
they won't have the time to figure out

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.190
if it's really a legitimate company

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:57.580
and there is even risk of fraud.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.800
- [Narrator] To understand
why investors are rushing

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.900
to blank-check companies
despite the potential risks,

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.507
first, you need to
understand how they work.

00:01:05.507 --> 00:01:08.880
(gentle music)

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.990
SPACs are public shell companies

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:13.070
that exist for a single purpose,

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:16.440
finding a private company and
taking it to market quickly.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.240
In order to take a company public,

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.830
a SPAC will merge with it.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.400
The company then gets
a spot on the exchange

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:23.880
and a new ticker.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.850
The SPAC transforms
into the target company

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.210
taking on its name.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.170
Once the deal closes,
investors who own shares

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:34.380
in the blank-check company now
own a piece of the new entity

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.580
and the founders of the SPAC
get a large part of it too,

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.850
often at a very good price.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.070
Blank-check companies are primarily owned

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:45.040
by veteran corporate
leaders and big investors,

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.600
like private equity firms.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.350
Usually it's a smaller amount
of shares that are available

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:50.320
to the public.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.760
- Ideally, a SPAC gives you
an opportunity to invest

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:57.370
in a good management team
or in a good entrepreneur,

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.280
where you have a good feeling about them

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.750
doing some sort of profitable deal

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:04.630
that increases the
value of the investment.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:07.170
But it is writing a blank-check,
you're taking a risk,

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:08.940
you don't know if they're
gonna do a good deal

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.080
and have the stock go up

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:11.350
or if they're gonna do a bad deal

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:12.490
and the stock will go down.

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:14.010
- [Narrator] For the
companies going public,

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.570
SPACs offer a different
approach than traditional IPOs,

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.870
where the private company
works with underwriters

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:22.190
or investment banks to work out a deal

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:24.220
and then takes the company public.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:25.770
The process can take months,

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:28.000
and under traditional IPO rules,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.660
the private company must stay quiet

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:31.540
and refrain from promoting its shares

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.150
until they start trading.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:36.030
This is where SPACs have a big advantage.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.980
- With a SPAC deal, companies
can actually talk about

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:41.480
themselves and promote
themselves to investors

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.060
in a way that they can't with an IPO.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.540
- [Narrator] Analysts say
growing mainstream acceptance

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.120
of SPACs, help fuel this year's boom,

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.640
but it was also the coronavirus pandemic.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.070
- We are looking at another
day of extreme volatility,

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:57.150
stocks opened just moments ago

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:00.000
with the Dow down more than 600 points.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.540
- [Narrator] This is because
during volatile markets,

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.320
a company's valuation can crash overnight.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.680
- The main advantage to
going public through a SPAC

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.450
has been definitely true this year

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.430
is there's more certainty of execution.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.190
You know earlier on in the process

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:14.630
they'll get the deal done.

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:17.810
IPOs get derailed at the
last minute all the time.

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.860
Because the markets are volatile,

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:21.890
they're falling, nobody
wants to go public.

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:23.770
- [Narrator] And this
year, many of the companies

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:27.220
going public through SPACs
are seeing big valuations.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:30.980
In September, United Wholesale
Mortgage agreed to go public

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:35.860
in the biggest SPAC deal ever
with a $16 billion valuation.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:39.460
This is a sharp change from
the early days in the 1980s

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:41.670
when the precursors of today's SPACs

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:43.350
were called blind pools.

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:47.140
These had a shady reputation
tied to Penny-Stock frauds.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:50.590
But in the decades since,
new laws and regulations

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:54.550
helped add credibility and
bolster investor confidence.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:58.940
Now as SPACs gain momentum,
more blue chip institutions

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:01.560
and veteran investors are buying in.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:02.810
- For instance, Goldman Sachs

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:05.320
never used to underwrite SPAC IPOs.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:08.313
A few years ago, it
did its first SPAC IPO.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:12.450
The New York Stock Exchange
now lists a lot of SPAC.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.160
For a long time it did not.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.160
- [Narrator] And with
SPAC funds getting larger,

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:19.860
the size of blank-check deals
are expected to grow as well.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:23.070
- This year Bill Ackman
launched the biggest SPAC ever.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:26.940
It could potentially take a
very, very large company public.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:31.010
- Our thesis here is by having
a $5 million dollar cash pile

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:32.420
in a public company.

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:34.690
It's really, it's our
own version of a unicorn.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:37.190
- [Narrator] Still, some
critics remain concerned that

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.540
companies going public through SPACs

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:41.120
aren't getting as much scrutiny

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:43.960
as those using traditional IPOs.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:46.950
- Exhibit A for that might
be the situation with Nikola,

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.940
which went public through
a SPAC a few months ago,

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:53.100
and has turned out to be the center

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:55.490
of a firestorm of fraud allegations.

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:58.170
- Nikola shares are tumbling
after a short seller

00:04:58.170 --> 00:04:59.157
called the company quote,

00:04:59.157 --> 00:05:02.670
"An intricate fraud
built on dozens of lies."

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:04.670
- The people who don't like SPACs

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:07.280
and prefer traditional IPOs would tell you

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:09.660
that if it had gone
through a traditional IPO,

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.130
there would have been
more time for investors

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:15.200
to kick the tires and to
scrutinize the company for fraud

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:16.630
before it went public.

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:19.270
- [Narrator] Federal
regulators are raising concerns

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:20.103
as well.

00:05:20.103 --> 00:05:23.460
The SEC is now examining
how blank-check companies

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.090
disclose their ownership,

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:27.660
and how compensation is
tied to an acquisition.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.790
- [Clayton] We wanna
make sure that investors

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.530
understand those things.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:35.530
And then at the time of the
transaction, when they vote,

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:39.990
that they're getting the
same rigorous disclosure

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:42.950
that you get in connection
with bringing an IPO to market.

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:44.236
- [Narrator] Critics also say,

00:05:44.236 --> 00:05:48.080
SPACs have often been a bad
deal for companies going public.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.800
- The founders of the SPAC,

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.480
when they do a deal, they
get to have a 20% stake

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:55.670
in the company that results from the deal

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:57.210
without having to pay very much for it.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:59.410
And so essentially a way they
can line their own pockets.

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:01.690
- [Narrator] But this
is starting to change.

00:06:01.690 --> 00:06:03.430
Akckman's blank-check company

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:06.040
is one of the largest to
not include this feature.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:07.580
- If Bill Ackman SPAC succeeds

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.630
in getting a large multibillion
dollar private company

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:12.340
and taking it public,

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.920
I think it'll be the
best proof of concept yet

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:16.510
for the SPAC idea.

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:17.343
- [Narrator] But right now,

00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:20.620
the future of blank-check
companies remains unclear.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:23.360
Investors say SPAC deals are here to stay,

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:26.600
while critics argue that the
blank-check boom is just that

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:29.402
a trend that isn't destined to last.

00:06:29.402 --> 00:06:31.985
(gentle music)

